<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gowa Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1906 CE (approx.) · capital Somba Opu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Makassar kingdom that built a great free port in South Sulawesi and fought the Dutch East India Company until the Treaty of Bongaya in 1667.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gowa was one of several Makassar chiefdoms in southwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>. In the early 16th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumaparisi-kallonna">Tumapa'risi' Kallonna</a> joined it with neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tallo-kingdom">Tallo</a> in a twin kingdom and began conquering nearby states. The fortified port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somba-opu">Somba Opu</a> became its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Makassar grew into one of the busiest ports in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, open to Malay, Portuguese, English and Danish traders and a key outlet for Moluccan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control. In 1605 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> and his <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tallo-kingdom">Tallo</a> chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaeng-matoaya">Karaeng Matoaya</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Gowa forced it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> states. Court scholars kept detailed chronicles and diaries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-speelman">Cornelis Speelman</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arung-palakka">Arung Palakka of Bone</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-hasanuddin">Sultan Hasanuddin</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bongaya">Treaty of Bongaya</a> of 1667 ended Gowa's power; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somba-opu">Somba Opu</a> fell in 1669. The sultanate survived as a lesser state until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> conquest of 1906.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gowa-sultanate.jpg" alt="Gowa Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sultanate_of_Gowa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1906 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Somba Opu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tumapa'risi' Kallonna, Alauddin, Hasanuddin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Makassarese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Gowa, Makassar, Gowa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1603&amp;focus=gowa-sultanate" aria-label="Gowa Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7992 2372 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gowa Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8312 2628L8208 2754M8312 2628L8281 2750M8312 2628L8311 2625M8312 2628L7959 2663M8312 2628L7959 2663M8312 2628L8337 2603M8312 2628L8333 2588"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8208 2754h0M8281 2750h0M8311 2625h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2663h0M8337 2603h0M8333 2588h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8312 2628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8312 2628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1603&amp;focus=gowa-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

